## What React protects

```jsx
const comment = '<img src=x onerror="steal()">';
<p>{comment}</p>          // rendered as visible text, nothing executes
```

Every interpolated string is escaped. This closes the single largest XSS category for free.

---

## Hole 1 — dangerouslySetInnerHTML

```jsx
// ❌ Executes whatever is in the string
<div dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: userContent }} />

// ✅ Sanitise with an allowlist
import DOMPurify from 'dompurify';
const clean = DOMPurify.sanitize(userContent, {
  ALLOWED_TAGS: ['b', 'i', 'em', 'strong', 'a', 'p', 'ul', 'li'],
  ALLOWED_ATTR: ['href'],
});
<div dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: clean }} />
```

Note: `<script>` inserted via `innerHTML` does **not** execute — but `onerror`, `onload` and `javascript:` URLs do. "I stripped script tags" is not sanitisation.

---

## Hole 2 — URLs

```jsx
// ❌ javascript: URLs execute on click
<a href={userProvidedUrl}>Link</a>

// ✅ Validate the protocol
function safeUrl(url) {
  try {
    const parsed = new URL(url, window.location.origin);
    return ['http:', 'https:', 'mailto:'].includes(parsed.protocol) ? parsed.href : '#';
  } catch {
    return '#';
  }
}
```

Same applies to `src`, `formAction` and `style` with `url()`.

---

## Hole 3 — spreading untrusted objects

```jsx
// ❌ If the API response ever contains dangerouslySetInnerHTML, you just injected HTML
<div {...apiResponse} />
```

Never spread data you did not construct onto an element.

---

## Defence in depth — Content Security Policy

```http
Content-Security-Policy:
  default-src 'self';
  script-src 'self' 'nonce-{random}';
  object-src 'none';
  base-uri 'self';
```

CSP means that even if injection succeeds, the injected script has no permission to run. `'unsafe-inline'` in `script-src` defeats the entire purpose — use nonces or hashes.

---

## Stored vs reflected vs DOM-based

| Type | Where the payload lives | Example |
|------|-------------------------|---------|
| **Stored** | Your database | A malicious comment served to every viewer |
| **Reflected** | The URL | `?search=<script>` echoed into the page |
| **DOM-based** | Client-side only | `innerHTML = location.hash` |

Stored is the most damaging — it hits every user who views the content.

---

## What an attacker gets

Running in your origin, they can read `localStorage` and non-`httpOnly` cookies, call your API with the user's session, rewrite the page to phish credentials, and keylog. This is why token storage and XSS are the same conversation.

## Why not the implicit flow

The old implicit flow returned the access token directly in the URL fragment — visible in browser history, referrer headers and any script on the page. It is deprecated. **Authorization code + PKCE is the current recommendation for all browser and mobile clients.**

---

## The flow

```text
1. App generates:
     code_verifier  = random 43–128 char string
     code_challenge = base64url(SHA256(code_verifier))

2. Redirect to provider with code_challenge (the HASH goes out)

3. User authenticates and consents

4. Provider redirects back with ?code=…   (short-lived, single use)

5. App POSTs code + code_verifier (the ORIGINAL) to the token endpoint

6. Provider hashes the verifier, compares to the stored challenge,
   and returns access + refresh tokens if they match
```

An attacker who intercepts the code in step 4 cannot use it — they do not have the verifier, which never left the client.

---

## Generating the challenge

```js
function base64url(buffer) {
  return btoa(String.fromCharCode(...new Uint8Array(buffer)))
    .replace(/\+/g, '-').replace(/\//g, '_').replace(/=+$/, '');
}

async function createPkcePair() {
  const bytes = crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(32));
  const verifier = base64url(bytes);
  const digest = await crypto.subtle.digest('SHA-256', new TextEncoder().encode(verifier));
  return { verifier, challenge: base64url(digest) };
}
```

Store the verifier in `sessionStorage` for the duration of the redirect — it is short-lived and single-use.

---

## The `state` parameter — do not skip it

```js
const state = crypto.randomUUID();
sessionStorage.setItem('oauth_state', state);
// …include state in the authorize URL…

// On return:
if (params.get('state') !== sessionStorage.getItem('oauth_state')) {
  throw new Error('State mismatch — possible CSRF');
}
```

`state` prevents CSRF on the callback: an attacker cannot make your app process *their* authorization code. Omitting it is a real vulnerability and a common review finding.

---

## OAuth vs OIDC

| | OAuth 2.0 | OpenID Connect |
|---|-----------|----------------|
| Purpose | **Authorisation** — access to resources | **Authentication** — who the user is |
| Returns | Access token | Access token + **ID token** (a JWT) |
| Analogy | A hotel key card | A passport |

"Log in with Google" is OIDC. OAuth alone tells you the app may access an API — it does not tell you who the user is. Confusing the two is the most common mistake on this question.

---

## The pragmatic note

> "For a web app with a backend, the BFF pattern is safer — the server completes the exchange and holds the tokens, setting an `httpOnly` session cookie. The browser never sees a token at all. I would only run PKCE fully client-side for a genuine SPA with no backend."

## The race

A page fires five requests at once. The token has expired, so all five return 401. Naive handling triggers five refreshes. With refresh-token rotation, the first invalidates the token the other four are using — and the user is logged out.

---

## The fix — one shared promise

```js
let refreshPromise = null;

async function refreshAccessToken() {
  // Everyone who arrives while a refresh is in flight awaits the SAME promise
  refreshPromise ??= (async () => {
    try {
      const res = await fetch('/auth/refresh', {
        method: 'POST',
        credentials: 'include',      // refresh token rides in the httpOnly cookie
      });
      if (!res.ok) throw new Error('Refresh failed');
      const { accessToken } = await res.json();
      setAccessToken(accessToken);
      return accessToken;
    } finally {
      refreshPromise = null;         // reset so the NEXT expiry can refresh again
    }
  })();

  return refreshPromise;
}
```

The `finally` reset is essential — without it, the second expiry an hour later would resolve the stale cached promise.

---

## Wiring it into the fetch layer

```js
async function apiFetch(url, options = {}, { isRetry = false } = {}) {
  const res = await fetch(url, {
    ...options,
    headers: { ...options.headers, Authorization: `Bearer ${getAccessToken()}` },
    credentials: 'include',
  });

  if (res.status !== 401 || isRetry) return res;   // ← isRetry prevents a loop

  try {
    await refreshAccessToken();
  } catch {
    logout();
    redirectToLogin();
    throw new Error('Session expired');
  }

  return apiFetch(url, options, { isRetry: true }); // retry ONCE
}
```

The `isRetry` guard is the second critical detail. Without it, a persistently failing endpoint retries forever.

---

## Proactive refresh — better UX

Refreshing *after* a 401 means every user occasionally pays a double round trip. Refresh slightly before expiry instead:

```js
function scheduleRefresh(expiresAt) {
  const delay = expiresAt - Date.now() - 60_000;   // 60s of headroom
  if (delay <= 0) return void refreshAccessToken();
  setTimeout(refreshAccessToken, delay);
}
```

Also refresh on `visibilitychange` when a tab becomes active again — a laptop that slept for two hours has a long-expired token.

---

## Multi-tab

Five open tabs each refresh independently. With rotation, they invalidate each other. Coordinate via `BroadcastChannel`:

```js
const channel = new BroadcastChannel('auth');
channel.onmessage = (e) => {
  if (e.data.type === 'token-refreshed') setAccessToken(e.data.token);
  if (e.data.type === 'logout') hardLogout();
};
```

This also gives you logout-everywhere for free, which users expect.

---

## The checklist

1. One shared in-flight promise
2. Retry each request exactly once
3. Refresh failure means logout, not a loop
4. Refresh proactively before expiry
5. Coordinate across tabs
6. Never refresh on a 403 — that is authorisation, not expiry

## Permissions, not roles

```jsx
// ❌ Roles hardcoded at every call site — adding a role means editing 50 files
{user.role === 'admin' || user.role === 'editor' ? <EditButton /> : null}

// ✅ Capability check — the role→permission mapping lives in one place
{can('post:edit') && <EditButton />}
```

When the product adds a "Moderator" role, only the mapping changes. This is the single most useful structural decision in the answer.

---

## Implementation

```jsx
const PermissionContext = createContext(null);

export function PermissionProvider({ user, children }) {
  const value = useMemo(() => {
    const permissions = new Set(user?.permissions ?? []);
    return {
      can: (permission) => permissions.has(permission),
      canAny: (list) => list.some((p) => permissions.has(p)),
      canAll: (list) => list.every((p) => permissions.has(p)),
    };
  }, [user]);

  return <PermissionContext.Provider value={value}>{children}</PermissionContext.Provider>;
}

export function usePermissions() {
  const ctx = useContext(PermissionContext);
  if (!ctx) throw new Error('usePermissions must be used within PermissionProvider');
  return ctx;
}
```

The server sends the resolved permission list, so the client never computes authorisation logic itself.

---

## Resource-level checks

Global permissions are not enough — "can edit posts" is different from "can edit *this* post":

```jsx
function canEditPost(user, post) {
  if (user.permissions.includes('post:edit:any')) return true;
  return user.permissions.includes('post:edit:own') && post.authorId === user.id;
}
```

Most real bugs live here, not in the role check.

---

## Route guards

```jsx
function RequirePermission({ permission, children }) {
  const { can } = usePermissions();
  const { user, isLoading } = useAuth();

  if (isLoading) return <Spinner />;                       // do not bounce too early
  if (!user) return <Navigate to="/login" replace />;
  if (!can(permission)) return <Navigate to="/403" replace />;
  return children;
}
```

---

## Hide vs disable

| Approach | Use when |
|----------|----------|
| **Hide** | The user could never have this — avoids advertising features they cannot buy |
| **Disable with a reason** | They could have it — upsell, or explain what is needed |

A disabled button with no explanation is the worst option. If you disable it, say why: *"Requires an Editor role — ask your admin."*

---

## The line that must be said

> "None of this is security. The bundle is on the user's machine; they can edit state in DevTools and render any component. Every endpoint authorises independently — the frontend checks exist so people are not shown things that will fail."

A candidate who presents client-side RBAC as protection is a genuine red flag.

---

## Also worth mentioning

Hiding a UI element does not hide the data if it already arrived in the payload. Filter server-side — do not send fields the user may not see and then hide them in the component.

## The rule

```jsx
// ❌ Card data in your state, your DOM, your network requests.
// This puts you in the strictest PCI DSS scope and is a serious liability.
const [cardNumber, setCardNumber] = useState('');
<input value={cardNumber} onChange={(e) => setCardNumber(e.target.value)} />
```

```jsx
// ✅ Stripe Elements — an iframe on Stripe's origin.
// The number never enters your DOM, your state, or your server.
import { CardElement, useStripe, useElements } from '@stripe/react-stripe-js';

function CheckoutForm({ clientSecret }) {
  const stripe = useStripe();
  const elements = useElements();

  async function handleSubmit(e) {
    e.preventDefault();

    const { error, paymentIntent } = await stripe.confirmCardPayment(clientSecret, {
      payment_method: { card: elements.getElement(CardElement) },
    });

    if (error) return setError(error.message);
    if (paymentIntent.status === 'succeeded') onSuccess();
  }

  return <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}><CardElement /><button>Pay</button></form>;
}
```

Because the fields live in a cross-origin iframe, your JavaScript **cannot** read them — which is exactly the point. Even XSS on your page cannot harvest card numbers.

---

## The amount is decided server-side

```jsx
// ❌ Never trust a client-supplied amount
fetch('/api/charge', { body: JSON.stringify({ amount: 100, item: 'pro-plan' }) });
```

Anyone can change that to `1`. The server looks up the price for `pro-plan`, creates the PaymentIntent, and returns only the `clientSecret`. The client never states what something costs.

---

## Idempotency

```js
// Retrying a payment that timed out must not charge twice
headers: { 'Idempotency-Key': orderId }
```

A `POST /payments` that times out may have succeeded. The idempotency key lets the provider deduplicate. Also disable the submit button on the first click and keep it disabled — double-submission is the most common real cause of duplicate charges.

---

## Confirm on the server, not the client

```text
❌ Client says "payment succeeded" → server marks the order paid
✅ Provider webhook → server verifies the signature → marks the order paid
```

A client redirect can be interrupted, replayed or forged. The webhook is the source of truth, and its signature must be verified. Say this — it is frequently the point of the question.

---

## 3D Secure

SCA in Europe means many payments require an additional challenge. The provider's SDK handles the redirect or modal, but your UI must handle the `requires_action` state rather than treating anything that is not `succeeded` as a failure.

---

## The rest

| Concern | Handling |
|---------|----------|
| PCI scope | Hosted fields keep you in SAQ A, the lightest tier |
| CSP | `frame-src` and `connect-src` must allow the provider |
| Errors | Show the provider's message — "card declined" is actionable, "error" is not |
| Retries | Preserve the form state; making them re-enter everything loses sales |
| Test mode | Separate keys, and make the environment visibly obvious |
